Based on a generic quantum geometric treatment of many-body interactions, we here show that any scalar boson maps onto an anyonic theory of the form  
\begin{equation}
    \eta_z\eta_{z^\prime}=e^{i2\pi q_{zz^\prime}/p}\eta_{z^\prime}\eta_z
\end{equation}
where $z$ is the lattice index, $p$ is an integer, and $q$ is an integer-valued matrix of essentially \textit{arbitrary} shape (subject only to the antisymmetry constraint, $q^T=-q$). Conventional identical anyons have $q_{zz^\prime}=q$, i.e., the exchange statistics are the same for all $z>z^\prime$ (and $-q$ for $z<z^\prime$). For instance, $q=1$ and $p=2$ yield a regular fermionic lattice. As soon as $q$ depends nontrivially on the lattice index, we obtain a generic network of \textit{non-identical} anyons.

Interpreting the scalar boson as a plasmonic field carrying charges, anyons are commonly associated with charge fractionalisation (e.g., in the fractional quantum Hall effect). Crucially, in our setup, charge quantization is built in from the get-go, and the resulting anyons carry fractional \textit{flux} rather than fractional charge. As a consequence, charge-conserving processes permit generic interaction terms of the form $\eta_z^m \eta_{z'}^{m'}$, substantially enlarging the class of allowed anyon couplings compared with more familiar constructions. Moreover, upon including a trivial node $z_0$, for which $\eta_{z_0}=\mathbf{1}_{z_0}$, the effective low-energy Hamiltonian can contain terms of the form $\mathbf{1}_{z_0}\eta_z+\mathrm{h.c.}$, corresponding to an unpaired anyon and thereby breaking the Wigner superselection rule (valid in the here considered non-relativistic limit).

