\noindent{\bf Example: SPT phases with non-invertible 1-form symmetry.}
A non-invertible 1-form symmetry in 2+1d is generally described by a braided fusion 1-category $\mathcal{B}$.
Its condensation completion is $\Mod(\mathcal{B})$, the 2-category of $\mathcal{B}$-module categories.
It is known that a fusion 2-category $\Mod(\mathcal{B})$ is group-theoretical if and only if $\mathcal{B} = \Rep(G)$ for some $G$ \cite[Theorem 3.1.3]{Decoppet:2023bay}, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
\Mod(\mathcal{B}) = \Mod(\Rep(G)) \cong 2\Rep(G).
\end{equation}
The fiber 2-functor of $2\Rep(G)$ is unique and is given by the forgetful functor, which corresponds to $(K, \lambda) = (\{e\}, 1)$.
Physically, this means that there is no non-trivial SPT phase with only a 1-form symmetry.

