A more general class of Lifshitz models based on free fields is given by the Lagrangian 
\begin{equation}\label{LagrLifshitz}
L=\frac{1}{2}\int \left[ i^m \phi \,(\partial_t^m\phi) -\alpha^2 \left(\partial_x^{n}\phi\right)^2\right]\,{\rm d}x\ ,
\end{equation}
which for $m=2$ reproduces the Hamiltonian \eqref{hamLifshitz} with $z=n$. More generally, the Lagrangian \eqref{LagrLifshitz} has a fractional dynamical exponent $z=2n/m$ for (positive) integers $n$ and $m$. However, for $m>2$, the theory contains higher time derivatives, which implies the quantization of the model, and consequently the study of EE, becomes more difficult and obscure. Thus, we restrict ourselves to the case where $m=2$, $z=n$, or equivalently the Hamiltonian \eqref{hamLifshitz}, for the remainder of this paper.

